    <h2>Which test to do</h2>
    <p>All three are valid and all three get you to the same place. The practical difference is what each one gives back:</p>
    <p><strong>30 minutes all-out.</strong> The most complete, and the one I recommend. It gives you FTP and LTHR from a single ride: average power for the 30 minutes is your FTP, and average heart rate for the last 20 is your LTHR. It's also the hardest to pace.</p>
    <p><strong>20 minutes all-out.</strong> Shorter and more repeatable. FTP comes from 95% of average power, because a 20-minute effort is held above true threshold. LTHR comes from average heart rate over the last 15 minutes.</p>
    <p><strong>2 × 8 minutes.</strong> Two maximal blocks with recovery between. FTP is 90% of the average of both. <strong>This test does not give you heart-rate zones</strong>: the blocks are too short for heart rate to settle where it needs to be. If you want HR zones, you need a continuous 20 or 30 minute effort.</p>

    <h2>Power or heart rate — which one leads?</h2>
    <p>If you have both, power leads. Heart rate lags behind the effort and moves with heat, sleep, caffeine and the stress of the day — two identical sessions can produce different heart rates with nothing at all having changed in your fitness. Power measures the work you're doing, directly.</p>
    <p>That doesn't make HR useless: it's the best backup there is when you don't have a meter, and comparing the two across a block tells you things neither one says alone.</p>

    <h2>How to do the test properly</h2>
    <p>Rested, fed, chest strap on — a wrist sensor won't do for this. On the trainer, or on a stretch of road with no traffic lights, no long descents and no traffic forcing you to soft-pedal. Warm up seriously: 15 or 20 minutes with a couple of short accelerations before the block starts.</p>
    <p>Go out even. The most common mistake is starting at the power you'd like to have and dragging yourself through the last eight minutes: the average comes out similar, but the test stops describing your threshold and starts describing your capacity to suffer badly distributed.</p>
  </div>
</section>
